<xri version="1.0">
   <description>
      <title>NukeX — Distribution-Fitted Stacking with Calibrated Colour Science</title>
      <copyright>Copyright (c) 2026 Scott Carter</copyright>
   </description>
   <platform os="linux" arch="x64" version="1.8.0:1.9.9">
      <package fileName="20260910-linux-x64-NukeX.tar.gz" sha1="950f2d2c9a5a04db26ec61d9afbc79bed13f3f84" type="module" releaseDate="20260910">
         <title>NukeX</title>
         <description>NukeX v5.0.4.1 - colour-science overhaul. Filter taxonomy from FITS FILTER/BAYERPAT/INSTRUME; quantum-efficiency database (55 cameras, 97 filters) driving a Q-matrix decomposition of dual-narrowband OSC stacks into Ha/OIII/SII; Lab/LCH ColorComposer with a calibrated emission-line palette, delivered in a NEW NukeX_composed window (on dual-narrowband data the stacked and stretched windows are unchanged from v4); OSC-as-LRGB luminance synthesis; QE override file picker; signed runtime updates for the camera database. The voxel record is now sized to the stack&apos;s real channel count, cutting a 24 MP OSC stack from 35.2 GB to 11.5 GB, and the alignment reference is checked before use. A batch may carry only one mono filter; stack L/R/G/B separately. Rating DB migrates in place. Package now ships share/qe_database.json beside bin/. 5.0.0.1 refuses a batch that mixes Bayer and mono frames, which was decoded wrongly whichever kind came first, and no longer terminates PixInsight when a frame cannot be measured. 5.0.0.2 adds the HaO3S2 three-line canonical filter so quad-band glass such as Optolong L-Quad Enhance stacks (FILTER=Lqef and friends), stops blaming the install for unrelated failures, fixes garbled punctuation in the interface, and reports its own version. 5.0.1.0 offers to learn a filter it does not recognise: it asks which emission lines the filter passes, records the answer in &lt;user-data&gt;/nukex4/filter_aliases.json, and re-stacks. 5.0.2.0 stacks LRGB mono batches, which used to be refused outright; rescues frames from the ends of a long session by chaining alignment through a neighbour when they cannot reach the reference; gives narrowband its own luminance so an emission pixel&apos;s colour comes from its line ratio rather than its brightness; stops averaging absent samples in as darkness at the frame edges; solves the stretch intensity against each image&apos;s own background; and caps the working batch by host RAM as well as VRAM. 5.0.3.0 solves a SHADOW POINT as well as an intensity, which is what turns a correct but flat auto-stretch into one with contrast -- on a 74-frame stack the separation between background and highlights goes from 0.040 to 0.299, and on the mono regression corpus from 0.156 to 0.466, with the background landing on the same target as before. It matches the colour channels&apos; sky levels, so a broadband stack is no longer green: that cast is an additive pedestal no downstream stretch can remove, measured at 1.54 to 1 in the background of a stack whose signal was already neutral to within 7 per cent. And it crops the stack to the rectangle every frame contributed to, taking away the thin-coverage border whose noise ran 3 to 5 times the interior. The Process Console and the FITS header (NUKEXBGM, NUKEXTRM) record exactly what was subtracted and what was kept. 5.0.4.0 corrects for the sky changing during a session. Frame sky level and spread are measured per colour channel and every frame is brought onto a common level before it is stacked, so a per-frame effect is no longer modelled per-pixel by the distribution fit. Measured on real sessions, stacked pixel-scale noise fell 7.5% on a 24-frame blue set and 76.5% on a 65-frame luminance set -- the latter worth roughly eighteen times the exposure. A session whose sky held still is left bit-for-bit unchanged. 5.0.4.1 fixes the stacked and noise images opening as a white crosshatch: a colour stack carries four planes -- red, green, blue and a synthesized luminance -- and PixInsight treats any plane past the third as transparency, so the luminance was being drawn as a checkerboard. Those windows now open as a proper colour image with any extra slot in its own window beside it. The auto-stretch also no longer leaves the sky at a quarter brightness: the default sky level drops from 0.25 to 0.12, which raises colour saturation 1.34x to 1.72x on real sessions and clips nothing at either end. It remains a control in the interface. 5.0.4.3 measures the stack&apos;s own noise instead of only predicting it: a new NukeX_measured_noise window shows the scatter the finished stack actually has, and the console reports measured, predicted and their ratio. The local noise figure no longer counts smooth structure as noise, one bad sample no longer inflates a pixel&apos;s across-frame noise estimate, and a camera&apos;s GAIN menu setting is no longer mistaken for electronic gain. 5.0.5.0 removes the fixed-pattern sky TILT from the stacked image: a flat plane fitted to the background with bright pixels ignored, the sky level left where it was, and a curved gradient deliberately untouched because a curved fit can subtract the faint outskirts of a large object. On a 65-frame set the tilt was six times the pixel noise corner to corner. Your stacked image changes with that release. 5.0.5.1 fixes the sky-tilt fit, which a bright object covering 30% or more of the frame could drag into subtracting real signal, and keeps it off the noisy frame rim; reports the tilt corner to corner; measures the stack&apos;s noise between pixels eight apart because adjacent pixels of a debayered, resampled stack are not independent (expect the reported noise ratio to rise); says which noise model ran; treats a ZWO or QHY GAIN as a menu setting whatever its value; makes the noise check&apos;s printed numbers agree; and adds the Remove sky gradient control that the 5.0.5.0 notes described. Your stacked image changes where the old fit was biased. 5.0.5.2 gives dual-narrowband stacks their colour back: the stretched image of an Ha/OIII stack had come out nearly grey since 5.0.3.2; emission-line stacks now stretch their lightness alone and compose the colour at the stretched lightness, with hue and saturation still taken from the linear line ratios. Broadband stacks are untouched. 5.0.5.3 fixes the narrowband hue at its root -- the line ratio is now taken on sky-subtracted line fluxes, so a faint Ha nebula is red, not a 50/50 mix -- and the palette no longer has a magenta between Ha and OIII; the Ha, OIII and SII planes open as their own windows; the voxel record is file-backed when it would crowd memory (a 24 MP stack kept 18 GB available instead of 6); and the default cache directory leaves /tmp, which is a RAM disk on most Linux systems. 5.0.6.0 makes the Huber M-estimator the default: measured on four real sessions the distribution model race produced a noisier stack on every one at thirteen to seventeen times the Phase B time; the race remains selectable under Options. Every stacked image changes. 5.0.6.1 stops sky noise being painted with the emission palette: colour now starts three sigma above the sky and is full at six, judged over a 7x7 neighbourhood so faint extended nebulosity keeps its colour and lone noise cannot.</description>
      </package>
   </platform>
</xri>
